# A noun phrase asked as a question and answered in the next sentence
# (`The result? A build that finishes in half the time.`) stages a
# reveal instead of stating the point. The fix states it (`The build
# finishes in half the time`).
#
# The list holds the nouns that set up a reveal. Nouns that end a real
# question stay out (`What's the difference?`, `What is the fix?`,
# `What was the outcome?`). A real question that ends on a listed noun
# still fires (`What was the result?`), since a rule cannot see the
# start of the sentence, but technical prose rarely asks one.
expr reveal [result, catch, upshot, kicker, verdict, takeaway, twist, payoff, culprit, trick, secret, downside, upside]
expr main [(the @reveal?), (the [best, worst] part?), (the [good, bad] news?), (the short answer?)]
let message "Staged reveal. State the point as a sentence instead of asking for it."
let description "Flags a noun phrase posed as a question and answered in the next sentence, such as The result? or The best part?"
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The result is a build that finishes in half the time."
allows "What's the difference between the two flags?"
